{{!-- =====================================================
     RAPID FIRE METHODOLOGY STORYBOARDING
===================================================== --}}

<main class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">

    {{!-- =================================================
         INTRODUCTION
    ================================================== --}}

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        METHODOLOGY
      </p>

      <h1>
        Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Build the work plan before you write it. Rapid Fire Methodology
        Storyboarding helps the proposal team turn the RFP scope into a
        logical, coordinated plan for delivering the project.
      </p>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         QUICK FACTS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-quick-facts"
    >

      <h2 id="methodology-quick-facts">
        At a Glance
      </h2>

      <div class="sasha-practice-fact-grid">

        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            PURPOSE
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Plan before writing
          </p>

          <p>
            Bring the proposal team together to develop the logic,
            activities, responsibilities, deliverables, and dependencies
            before anyone starts drafting methodology prose.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            BEST FOR
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Full Effort Pursuits
          </p>

          <p>
            Particularly valuable for competitive, multidisciplinary,
            technically complex pursuits where the methodology carries
            significant evaluation weight.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            OUTPUT
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            A proposal-ready work plan
          </p>

          <p>
            The session produces a structured methodology that Sasha can
            use as the foundation for writing the proposal section.
          </p>

        </article>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHY STORYBOARD FIRST
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-why"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHY IT MATTERS
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-why">
            The difficult work should happen before the writing.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            A methodology should not be a generic list of engineering
            activities or a rewritten version of the RFP scope.
          </p>

          <p>
            It should show the evaluator that the proposed team has
            thought carefully about how this particular project will
            actually be delivered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding gets that thinking out
            of people's heads and into one coordinated work plan before
            the proposal writer begins turning it into prose.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHO PARTICIPATES
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-participants"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHO PARTICIPATES
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-participants">
            Bring the people who will actually deliver the work.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          The objective is to capture the knowledge of the proposed team,
          not leave the methodology to one proposal writer working alone.
          Include the people who understand the scope, technical issues,
          interfaces, risks, schedule, and client.
        </p>


        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Proposed Project Manager
            </strong>

            <span>
              Leads the delivery thinking and helps establish how the
              project should progress from one stage to the next.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Technical Leads
            </strong>

            <span>
              Define the activities, technical decisions, risks,
              interfaces, inputs, and deliverables within their areas.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Discipline Leads
            </strong>

            <span>
              Identify where disciplines depend on one another and where
              coordination must occur.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Proposal Manager
            </strong>

            <span>
              Facilitates the session, checks the RFP scope, records the
              storyboard, and keeps the discussion moving.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Client-Facing Staff
            </strong>

            <span>
              Contribute knowledge about client expectations, working
              preferences, concerns, and important project context.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Specialists as Needed
            </strong>

            <span>
              Bring in environmental, stakeholder, construction,
              approvals, risk, operations, or other specialists where
              their work materially affects the methodology.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         START WITH THE RFP
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-start"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            START HERE
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-start">
            Begin with the RFP scope.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          Before the team invents its own structure, identify everything
          the client has asked the successful consultant to do.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Scope items</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Tasks and phases</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Deliverables</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Milestones</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Client responsibilities</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Approvals and decisions</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Meetings</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Schedule requirements</span>
          </div>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Account for the entire requested scope.
          </strong>

          <span>
            If something is missing, ambiguous, contradictory, or poorly
            defined, flag it. Do not quietly invent the missing work.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         BUILD THE WORK BREAKDOWN STRUCTURE
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-process"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            BUILD THE STORYBOARD
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-process">
            Work through each task systematically.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Scope Item
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify the requirement in the RFP that this task addresses.
              Every required scope item needs a clear home in the work plan.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Key Issues
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify the technical, operational, regulatory, stakeholder,
              environmental, schedule, financial, or project-management
              issues affecting this task.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Ask why this task could be difficult.
              </strong>

              <span>
                The answer helps turn a generic work plan into one designed
                for this project.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Objective
            </h3>

            <p>
              Define what the task must accomplish. An objective describes
              the result the team is trying to achieve, not the work it
              performs to get there.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Activities
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify what the team will actually do. Be specific enough
              that the evaluator can understand how the objective will be
              achieved.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Value-Added Elements
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify improvements to the basic approach that reduce risk,
              improve coordination, accelerate decisions, prevent rework,
              improve information, or otherwise create meaningful value.
            </p>

            <p class="sasha-practice-step-note">
              Value added does not simply mean doing more work.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Deliverables
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify what exists when the task is complete: a decision,
              design, recommendation, model, approval, report, drawing,
              memorandum, or other tangible output.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Personnel
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify who needs to participate and why. Include the
              disciplines and specialists whose involvement materially
              affects the work.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Timing and Dependencies
            </h3>

            <p>
              Determine when the task occurs, what information it needs,
              what it produces for later tasks, and what must happen before
              the next activity can begin.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         THREE IMPORTANT DISTINCTIONS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-distinctions"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
          KEEP THESE STRAIGHT
        </p>

        <h2 id="methodology-distinctions">
          Objective, activity, and deliverable are different things.
        </h2>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Objective
            </strong>

            <span>
              What does this task need to accomplish?
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Activity
            </strong>

            <span>
              What will the team actually do?
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Deliverable
            </strong>

            <span>
              What tangible result exists when the activity is complete?
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         CONNECT THE WORK
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-connections"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            SHOW THE FLOW
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-connections">
            Projects are not collections of independent tasks.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Show where one activity produces information needed by another,
            where decisions control downstream work, and where approvals
            or client input affect the sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make multidisciplinary interfaces visible. The client is hiring
            one project team, not a collection of isolated specialists.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the relationships become difficult to explain in prose,
            consider a workflow, schedule, process diagram, or other graphic.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         CLIENT + RISK + STRATEGY
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-strengthen"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            STRENGTHEN THE PLAN
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-strengthen">
            Make the methodology specific to this pursuit.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span